    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d86">

                <pb facs="0087" n="73" sameAs="frus:frus1947v06/pg73"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">851G.00/2–1647: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Caffery</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Paris</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1947-02-16T15:00:00+01:00">February 16,
                            1947—3 p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>705. I reviewed in some detail yesterday with Ramadier the situation in
                    Indochina, restating our point of view and emphasizing especially the outmoded
                    colonial outlook of many French officials, especially in Saigon. Ramadier
                    agreed; in fact, he agreed that our point of view was reasonable all along the
                    line. He told me that Leclerc has definitely turned down the Government’s
                    request to go to Indochina as High Commissioner and that he, Ramadier, had
                    offered the job only a few minutes before to General Juin,<anchor n="56"
                        xml:id="d86-f1"/> who promised to <pb facs="0088" n="74" xml:id="pg74"/>
                    think it over and reply at an early date. Ramadier observed: “Of course no one
                    wants that job at this juncture, but I very much hope that Juin will take it”.
                    (Juin came out of Ramadier’s office as I went in).<anchor n="57" xml:id="d86-f2"
                    />
                </p>
                <p>Repeated London 136.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Caffery</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d86-f1" n="56" xml:id="d86-f1-content">
                <p>Gen. Alphonse Pierre Juin, Chief of the French Army General Staff.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d86-f2" n="57" xml:id="d86-f2-content">
                <p>Telegram 839, February 24, 2 p.m., from Paris, reported that General Juin
                    declined the offer (851G.00/2–2447).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
